Sol's round 2 showed that A4's reference has no misplaced impl. FX0401 rejects it because crates/tide-admit/src/walk/traits.rs:86-90 gives a type-defining module only to a direct local ADT. An impl of a foreign trait for `&Local`, `&mut Local` or `Box<Local>`, such as the standard `impl IntoIterator for &Local` idiom behind `for x in &local`, therefore gets "no module can hold this impl" wherever it is placed. A reference-only fix cannot pass without changing A4's frozen interface.

The new decision is that FX0401 is too strict, so fix the checker. Its type-defining module looks through the fundamental wrappers (&T, &mut T, Box<T>, as in Rust's orphan rule) to the local ADT. An impl on such a wrapper then lives in the local type's exact module, as D4 ("Impls live in the module of the type or the trait") and reference §5.2 already say. The grounding is independent of the held-out set:
- D4 and §5.2's wording;
- reference §8's "also for &File";
- companion §18's `impl Bar for &Feed` treated as an impl applying to Feed;
- Rust's own coherence rules for fundamental types.

Only conformance/rules.md's REF-0401 gloss ("non-local self types use the trait module") narrows it, and it gets corrected. No reference, companion or diagnostic-text change. `impl From<Local> for u16` stays FX0401.

